From 4158906f2a5b3f202af1615fb80357a9a85987cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel JB Clark <djbclark@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 22:00:37 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Made CMDB loading all-or-nothing: one bad entry installs
 nothing

Replaces the per-entry skip of "Fixed one bad CMDB entry discarding
every entry in its section" (8f0076b81). A bad entry in host_specific.json
now rejects the whole file: the loader validates every section first
and installs nothing unless all of it is valid. A skipped entry used to
leave whatever it guarded (an interlock, for example) unguarded.

The loader keeps the pending classes and variables, installs them only
after every check has passed, and rolls back on a failed insertion. It
parses with JsonParseFileStrict() (libntech), which refuses a repeated
object member and any non-whitespace after the document. Null values
no longer crash it, and a dotted variable key gets a warning.

This is a squash of the fork's tendcf-integration cmdb loader work
(djbclark/core 4443504) onto the overlay base: libpromises/cmdb.c,
tests/unit/cmdb_test.c and tests/acceptance/00_basics/06_host_specific_data
are taken from that commit, plus the cmdb_test line in
tests/unit/Makefile.am. The one difference is the secrets-tracking
accessor: the base has EvalContextVariableGet(ctx, ref, type_out), so
EvalContextVariableGetPlaintext() is spelled that way here (three call
sites: one in cmdb.c, two in cmdb_test.c).

It includes the null-value crash fix (B-20, 87490bb8f) and the
dotted-key warning (B-7, ee1f9d60c) because the final cmdb.c contains
them. Requires libntech with JsonParseFileStrict() (fork PR
djbclark/libntech#11).
